## File to hold docstrings for the wrapped MUESLI types and functions.
##
## The bindings themselves are generated by CxxWrap at load time, so there is nowhere to
## attach documentation in the usual way; the empty `function ... end` declarations below give
## the docstrings a home without adding methods.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tensor types
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

@doc """
    Ivector(v::AbstractVector)
    Ivector(a, b, c)

A three-component vector, MUESLI's `ivector`.

Supports 1-based indexing, iteration and `convert(Vector{Float64}, v)`.

# Examples
```julia
v = Ivector([1.0, 2.0, 3.0])
v[2]                              # 2.0
convert(Vector{Float64}, v)
```
"""
Ivector

@doc """
    Itensor(m::AbstractMatrix)
    Itensor(a::AbstractVector, b::AbstractVector, c::AbstractVector)
    Itensor(a00, a01, a02, a10, a11, a12, a20, a21, a22)

A general second-order tensor in three dimensions, MUESLI's `itensor`.

# Details
The matrix constructor takes a `3 × 3` Julia matrix. Julia stores matrices column-major while
MUESLI's constructor is row-major, so the components are reordered on the way in — the round
trip is faithful and nothing is transposed. Any other shape raises an error.

Behaves like a `3 × 3` array: indexing and slicing (both 1-based), `axes`, `length`, `ndims`,
iteration, and `convert` to `Matrix{Float64}`. `copyto!(A, T)` and `copyto!(T, A)` move a whole
tensor in one call and do not allocate for `Float64` arrays, which is what to use in a hot loop. With
[Tensors.jl](https://github.com/Ferrite-FEM/Tensors.jl) loaded it also converts to
`Tensor{2, 3}`.

Finite strain material points take the deformation gradient as an `Itensor`, since it is not
symmetric.

# Examples
```julia
F = Itensor([1.05 0.02 0.0; 0.0 0.98 0.01; 0.0 0.0 1.01])
F[1, 2]                           # 0.02
F[1, :]                           # first row
convert(Matrix{Float64}, F)

A = zeros(3, 3)
copyto!(A, F)                     # no allocation
copyto!(F, 2 .* A)                # overwrite F in place
```

# See Also
- [`Istensor`](@ref) for the symmetric counterpart
"""
Itensor

@doc """
    Istensor(m::AbstractMatrix)
    Istensor(t00, t11, t22, t12, t20, t01)

A **symmetric** second-order tensor in three dimensions, MUESLI's `istensor`.

# Details
Only six components are stored. The matrix constructor reads the symmetric part of the `3 × 3`
matrix it is given, so a non-symmetric input is symmetrised rather than rejected.

`Istensor` is a subtype of [`Itensor`](@ref) on the C++ side, so it supports the same array
interface, and converts to `SymmetricTensor{2, 3}` when
[Tensors.jl](https://github.com/Ferrite-FEM/Tensors.jl) is loaded.

Small strain material points take the strain and return stresses as `Istensor`; in the finite
strain setting the second Piola-Kirchhoff, Kirchhoff and Cauchy stresses are `Istensor` while
the first Piola-Kirchhoff stress is a general [`Itensor`](@ref).

# Examples
```julia
ε = Istensor([0.001 0.0002 0.0; 0.0002 -0.0005 0.0001; 0.0 0.0001 0.0003])
σ = Istensor()                    # empty, to be filled by a bang method
```
"""
Istensor

@doc """
    Itensor4(a::AbstractArray{<:Real, 4})
    Itensor4()

A fourth-order tensor in three dimensions, MUESLI's `itensor4`.

# Details
The constructor takes a `3 × 3 × 3 × 3` array; anything else raises an error. The default
constructor gives an uninitialised tensor to be filled by a bang method such as
[`tangentTensor!`](@ref MuesliMaterials.tangentTensor!).

Indexing is 1-based in all four slots.

# Examples
```julia
ℂ = Itensor4()
tangentTensor!(mp, ℂ)
ℂ[1, 1, 2, 2]
```
"""
Itensor4

@doc """
    ArrayOfITensors()

A growable list of [`Itensor`](@ref) values, for MUESLI routines that take several tensors at
once.

Use `push!` to append a `3 × 3` Julia matrix, `clear!` to empty it and `size` for the count.
"""
ArrayOfITensors

@doc """
    ArrayOfIsTensors()

A growable list of [`Istensor`](@ref) values, for MUESLI routines that take several symmetric
tensors at once — the per-branch relaxation strains of a viscoelastic material, for instance.

Use `push!` to append a `3 × 3` Julia matrix, `clear!` to empty it and `size` for the count.
"""
ArrayOfIsTensors

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Material properties
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

@doc """
    MaterialProperties()

A map of named material parameters, MUESLI's `materialProperties`.

# Details
This is how MUESLI passes named parameters to a material constructor, and it is the only route
for models with many parameters. The backing store is a multimap, so setting the same key more
than once keeps every value — several models rely on that to receive a list.

# Examples
```julia
props = MaterialProperties()
setProperty!(props, "young", 210000.0)
setProperty!(props, "poisson", 0.3)

material = ElasticIsotropicMaterial(props)
```

# See Also
- [`setProperty!`](@ref MuesliMaterials.setProperty!), [`getProperty`](@ref MuesliMaterials.getProperty)
- [`setString!`](@ref MuesliMaterials.setString!), [`getString`](@ref MuesliMaterials.getString)
"""
MaterialProperties

@doc """
    setProperty!(props::MaterialProperties, key::String, value::Real)

Add a named numeric property.

Setting the same key more than once **keeps both values** rather than replacing the first, so
that models expecting a list of values receive one.

# See Also
- [`getProperty`](@ref MuesliMaterials.getProperty)
"""
function setProperty! end

@doc """
    getProperty(props::MaterialProperties, key::String)
    getProperty(material, name::PropertyName)

Read a property back.

From a [`MaterialProperties`](@ref MuesliMaterials.MaterialProperties) map, returns **all**
values stored under `key` as a `Vector{Float64}`; an unknown key gives an empty vector rather
than an error.

From a *material*, takes one of the `PR_*` constants — `PR_YOUNG`, `PR_POISSON`, `PR_LAMBDA`,
`PR_MU`, `PR_BULK`, `PR_YIELD` and so on — and returns that single value.

# Examples
```julia
getProperty(props, "young")                # [210000.0]
getProperty(material, PR_YOUNG)            # 210000.0
```
"""
function getProperty end

@doc """
    setString!(props::MaterialProperties, key::String, value::String)

Set a string-valued option.

# Details
MUESLI spells a flag as a bare `"key value"` entry in the property map rather than as a key
with a string value, so this concatenates the two. Read it back with
[`getString`](@ref MuesliMaterials.getString) and test for it with
[`hasKeyword`](@ref MuesliMaterials.hasKeyword).
"""
function setString! end

@doc """
    getString(props::MaterialProperties, key::String)

Read a string-valued option.

Returns `"NotFound"` if the key was never set — it does not raise.
"""
function getString end

@doc """
    hasKeyword(props::MaterialProperties, key::String)

Whether a string-valued option was set under `key`.
"""
function hasKeyword end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Materials
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

@doc """
    check(material)

Ask MUESLI whether the material's parameters are admissible.

Worth calling once after construction: an inadmissible parameter set is otherwise only noticed
much later, when a stress evaluation returns nonsense.

# Returns
`true` if the material is consistent.
"""
function check end

@doc """
    name(material)

The name MUESLI knows the material by, e.g. `"NeoHooke"`.
"""
function name end

@doc """
    density(material)
    density(mp)

The mass density of a material, or of the material a material point was created from.
"""
function density end

@doc """
    setDensity(material, ρ)

Set the mass density. Material points already created from `material` see the new value.
"""
function setDensity end

@doc """
    referenceTemperature(material)

The reference temperature of the material.
"""
function referenceTemperature end

@doc """
    setReferenceTemperature(material, θ)

Set the reference temperature. Material points already created from `material` see the new value.
"""
function setReferenceTemperature end

@doc """
    isVariational(material)

Whether the material is flagged for the variational formulation, see [`setVariational`](@ref MuesliMaterials.setVariational).
"""
function isVariational end

@doc """
    setVariational(material)

Flag the material for the variational formulation.
"""
function setVariational end

@doc """
    print(material)

Print MUESLI's own description of the material — its type and derived constants — to `stdout`.
"""
function print end

@doc """
    createMaterialPoint(material)

Create a material point for `material`.

Equivalent to calling the matching `...MP` constructor, e.g. `ElasticIsotropicMP(material)`.

The material point keeps `material` alive. MUESLI itself only holds a reference to it, so
without this the garbage collector could free a material that points still use.

# See Also
- [`updateCurrentState`](@ref MuesliMaterials.updateCurrentState)
"""
function createMaterialPoint end

@doc """
    ElasticIsotropicMaterial(E::Real, ν::Real, ρ::Real = 1.0)
    ElasticIsotropicMaterial(props::MaterialProperties)

Linear isotropic elasticity, MUESLI's `elasticIsotropicMaterial`.

# Arguments
- `E`: Young's modulus
- `ν`: Poisson's ratio
- `ρ`: density (default: `1.0`)
- `props`: alternatively a property map carrying `"young"` and `"poisson"`

# Details
The response is ``\\sigma = 2\\mu\\varepsilon + \\lambda\\,\\mathrm{tr}(\\varepsilon)\\,I``,
with the Lamé parameters derived from `E` and `ν`.

# Examples
```julia
material = ElasticIsotropicMaterial(210000.0, 0.3)
mp = ElasticIsotropicMP(material)
```

# See Also
- [`ElasticIsotropicMP`](@ref)
"""
ElasticIsotropicMaterial

@doc """
    ElasticAnisotropicMaterial(c::AbstractVector, ρ::Real = 1.0)
    ElasticAnisotropicMaterial(props::MaterialProperties)

Fully anisotropic linear elasticity, MUESLI's `elasticAnisotropicMaterial`.

# Arguments
- `c`: the **21** independent entries of the symmetric ``6 \\times 6`` stiffness matrix
  ``\\mathbb{C}`` (any other length raises an error); see below for the ordering
- `ρ`: density (default: `1.0`)
- `props`: alternatively a property map with the keys `c1111`, `c1122`, `c1133`, `c1123`, `c1113`, `c1112`, `c2222`, …,
  `c1212`, in the order of `c` below

# Details
MUESLI's Voigt convention numbers the symmetric strain/stress components as
``[11, 22, 33, 23, 13, 12]``, i.e. index 4 is the ``23`` shear, index 5 the ``13`` shear and
index 6 the ``12`` shear — **not** the ``[11, 22, 33, 12, 23, 13]`` ordering used by some other
tools. `c` fills the upper triangle of ``\\mathbb{C}`` row by row, starting from the diagonal
entry of each row:
```
c = [C11, C12, C13, C14, C15, C16,
          C22, C23, C24, C25, C26,
               C33, C34, C35, C36,
                    C44, C45, C46,
                         C55, C56,
                              C66]
```
so `c[1] == C11`, `c[2] == C12`, …, `c[7] == C22`, `c[8] == C23`, and so on down to
`c[21] == C66`; the matrix is completed by symmetry (``C_{ij} = C_{ji}``).

# See Also
- [`ElasticOrthotropicMaterial`](@ref), [`ElasticTransverselyisotropicMaterial`](@ref)
"""
ElasticAnisotropicMaterial

@doc """
    ElasticOrthotropicMaterial(c::AbstractVector, ρ::Real = 1.0)
    ElasticOrthotropicMaterial(props::MaterialProperties)

Orthotropic linear elasticity, MUESLI's `elasticOrthotropicMaterial`. A specialisation of
[`ElasticAnisotropicMaterial`](@ref) for a material with three mutually orthogonal planes of
material symmetry aligned with the coordinate axes; every stiffness entry coupling a normal and
a shear direction, or two different shear directions, is implicitly zero.

# Arguments
- `c`: the **9** independent stiffness constants (any other length raises an error), in the
  order
  ```
  c = [C11, C12, C13, C22, C23, C33, C44, C55, C66]
  ```
  using the same Voigt convention as [`ElasticAnisotropicMaterial`](@ref) — `C44`, `C55`, `C66`
  are the ``23``, ``13`` and ``12`` shear stiffnesses, respectively.
- `ρ`: density (default: `1.0`)
- `props`: alternatively a property map with the engineering constants `young1`, `young2`, `young3`,
  `poisson12`, `poisson13`, `poisson23`, `g23`, `g12` and `g13`
"""
ElasticOrthotropicMaterial

@doc """
    ElasticTransverselyisotropicMaterial(c::AbstractVector, ρ::Real = 1.0)
    ElasticTransverselyisotropicMaterial(E1::Real, E2::Real, ν12::Real, G23::Real, G12::Real, ρ::Real = 1.0)
    ElasticTransverselyisotropicMaterial(props::MaterialProperties)

Transversely isotropic linear elasticity, MUESLI's `elasticTransverselyisotropicMaterial`. A
specialisation of [`ElasticAnisotropicMaterial`](@ref) with the axis of symmetry fixed along
``x``, i.e. `[1, 0, 0]`: properties are isotropic in the transverse `y`-`z` plane, while `x` is
the distinguished (e.g. fibre) direction.

# Arguments
- `c`: the **6** independent stiffness constants (any other length raises an error), in the
  order
  ```
  c = [C11, C12, C22, C23, C44, C55]
  ```
  using the same Voigt convention as [`ElasticAnisotropicMaterial`](@ref). The remaining
  entries of ``\\mathbb{C}`` follow from transverse isotropy: ``C13 = C12``, ``C33 = C22`` and
  ``C66 = C55``.
- `E1`, `E2`: the axial (``x``) and transverse (`y`-`z`) Young's moduli, as an alternative to
  `c`
- `ν12`: the axial-transverse Poisson's ratio
- `G23`, `G12`: the transverse and axial-transverse shear moduli
- `ρ`: density (default: `1.0`)
- `props`: alternatively a property map with `young1`, `young2`, `poisson12`, `g23` and `g12`

# Details
In both forms the entries of ``\\mathbb{C}`` that are not set explicitly are zero.
"""
ElasticTransverselyisotropicMaterial

@doc """
    SplasticMaterial(E, ν, ρ, Hiso, Hkine, yield, xalpha, plasticityType::String)
    SplasticMaterial(props::MaterialProperties)

Rate-independent small strain plasticity with Young's modulus `E`, Poisson's ratio `ν`, density `ρ` and
the initial yield stress `yield`. `Hiso` and `Hkine` are the isotropic and kinematic hardening moduli.
The elastic energy is `K/2 (tr ε)² + μ ‖dev(ε - εᵖ)‖²`.

`plasticityType` selects the yield criterion:
- `"mises"`: `f = ‖dev(σ) - Q‖ - √(2/3) (yield - q)` with linear isotropic and kinematic hardening,
  `Wᵖ = Hiso/2 ξ² + Hkine/3 ‖Ξ‖²`.
- `"tresca"`: `f = σ₁ - σ₃ - (yield - q)` with the principal stresses `σ₁ ≥ σ₃` and linear isotropic hardening.
- `"drucker"`: `f = √J₂ + xalpha tr(σ) - (xalpha + √3/3)(yield - q)` with linear isotropic hardening;
  `xalpha` is the semi-angle of the cone and is not used by the other criteria.

Here `q` is the isotropic hardening stress and `Q` the kinematic one.
"""
SplasticMaterial

@doc """
    ViscoelasticMaterial(E, ν, ρ, nvisco::Integer, eta::AbstractVector, tau::AbstractVector)
    ViscoelasticMaterial(props::MaterialProperties)

Small strain viscoelasticity as a generalized Maxwell model with `nvisco` branches. `E` and `ν` are the long-term
(equilibrium) constants. The volumetric response is elastic and only the deviatoric part is viscous,
`dev(σ) = 2μ∞ e + Σₐ 2μₐ (e - eₐᵛ)` with `μ∞ = E/(2(1 + ν))` and the deviatoric strain `e`.

Branch `a` has the relaxation time `tau[a]` and the shear modulus `μₐ = eta[a]/(2 tau[a])`, so `eta` is a
viscosity, not a modulus. Its internal strain is advanced by implicit Euler, `eₐᵛ ← eₐᵛ + Δt/(Δt + τₐ) (e - eₐᵛ)`.

The `props` form uses `young`, `poisson` and one `eta` and `tau` entry per branch.
"""
ViscoelasticMaterial

@doc """
    ViscoplasticMaterial(; E, nu, yield, Hiso, Hkine, eta, alpha, exponent = 1.0, rateref = 1.0, rho = 1.0,
                         plasticity = "von_mises")
    ViscoplasticMaterial(props::MaterialProperties)
    ViscoplasticMaterial(λ, μ, ρ, Hiso, Hkine, yield, plasticityType::String, eta, alpha)

Rate-dependent small strain plasticity.

It uses the yield function of [`SplasticMaterial`](@ref) with linear isotropic (`Hiso`) and kinematic (`Hkine`)
hardening and the initial yield stress `yield`, regularized by a viscosity `eta`. The viscous law is a power law with
the exponent `exponent` and the reference rate `rateref`, and `alpha` is the thermal expansion coefficient.
`plasticity` is `"von_mises"` or `"tresca"`.

The `props` form uses the keys `young`, `poisson`, `density`, `yieldstress`, `isotropich`, `kinematich`, `eta`,
`alpha`, `viscousexponent` and `srateref`, plus the string `"model"`.

!!! warning
    Prefer the keyword form. The positional constructor is MUESLI's own: it takes the Lamé constants `λ` and `μ`
    instead of Young's modulus and Poisson's ratio, and it sets the exponent and the reference rate to zero, unlike
    the other forms, which default them to one.
"""
ViscoplasticMaterial

@doc """
    GTN_Material(E, ν, ρ, q1, q2, yield)

Gurson-Tvergaard-Needleman porous plasticity without hardening, with the damage variable `d`. The stress is scaled by
`1 - d`, and the yield function is `3/2 s² + 2 q1 fᵥ cosh(q2 t/2) - 1 - (q1 fᵥ)²` with the void fraction `fᵥ`, the
normalized deviatoric stress norm `s = ‖dev σ‖/yield` and the normalized trace `t = tr σ/yield`. `q1` and `q2` are the
Tvergaard parameters and `yield` is the yield stress of the matrix.

The `props` form uses `young`, `poisson`, `density`, `yieldstress`, `q1_gurson` and `q2_gurson`.
"""
GTN_Material

@doc """
    Gurson_Material(E, ν, ρ, xRinf, xRb, yield)

Gurson porous plasticity with isotropic hardening. The yield function is `J₂(dev σ) - a Y²/3` with
`a = 1 + f² - 2f cosh(3p/(2Y))`, where `f` is the void fraction (the damage variable `d` in MUESLI) and `p = tr(σ)/3`.
The yield stress `Y = yield + xRinf (1 - exp(-xRb R))` saturates at `yield + xRinf` with the rate `xRb`.

The `props` form uses `young`, `poisson`, `density`, `yieldstress`, `rinf_hardening` and `rb_hardening`.
"""
Gurson_Material

@doc """
    Lemaitre_Material(E, ν, ρ, r, s, yield, xR_inf, xR_b)

Lemaitre ductile damage with von Mises plasticity and saturating isotropic hardening. The stress is scaled by `1 - d`,
the yield function is `√(3/2) ‖dev σ‖/(1 - d) - σᵧ(R)` with `σᵧ = yield + xR_inf (1 - exp(-xR_b R))`, and the damage
grows as `ḋ = γ̇ (-Y/r)ˢ/(1 - d)`. `Y = -σᵧ²/(6G) - K εᵥ²/2` is the damage energy release rate with the shear
modulus `G`, the bulk modulus `K` and the elastic volumetric strain `εᵥ`. `r` is the denominator and `s` the
exponent of the damage law.

The `props` form uses `young`, `poisson`, `density`, `yieldstress`, `r_lemaitre`, `s_lemaitre`, `rinf_hardening`
and `rb_hardening`.
"""
Lemaitre_Material

@doc """
    LemKin_Material(E, ν, ρ, r, s, yield, xR_inf, xR_b, xa, xb)

Lemaitre damage with kinematic hardening.

This is [`Lemaitre_Material`](@ref) with an additional kinematic hardening of Armstrong-Frederick type: the back
stress `β` evolves as `β̇ = γ̇ (xa N - xb β)` with the flow direction `N`, where `xa` is the hardening coefficient
and `xb` the dynamic recovery.

The `props` form adds `akin_hardening` and `bkin_hardening` to the keys of [`Lemaitre_Material`](@ref).
"""
LemKin_Material

@doc """
    NeoHookeMaterial(props::MaterialProperties)

Compressible neo-Hookean hyperelasticity, MUESLI's `neohookeanMaterial`. The stored energy is
`W = λ/2 (ln J)² + μ/2 (I₁ - 3) - μ ln J` with `I₁ = tr C` and `J = det F`. At ``F = I`` the material is stress
free and stores no energy.

Build it from a property map carrying `"young"` and `"poisson"` (or `"lambda"` and `"mu"`). Setting the key
`"subtype regularized"` selects `W = K/2 (ln J)² + μ/2 (Ĩ₁ - 3)` with `Ĩ₁ = J^(-2/3) I₁` and the bulk modulus `K`.

# Examples
```julia
props = MaterialProperties()
setProperty!(props, "young", 210000.0)
setProperty!(props, "poisson", 0.3)

material = NeoHookeMaterial(props)
mp = NeoHookeMP(material)
```

# See Also
- [`NeoHookeMP`](@ref)
"""
NeoHookeMaterial

@doc """
    SVKMaterial(props::MaterialProperties)

Saint Venant-Kirchhoff hyperelasticity, the linear relation between the Green-Lagrange strain and the second
Piola-Kirchhoff stress with the stored energy `W = λ/2 (tr E)² + μ E:E`. Build it from a property map carrying
`"young"` and `"poisson"` (or `"lambda"` and `"mu"`).
"""
SVKMaterial

@doc """
    YeohMaterial(C1, C2, C3, bulk, compressible::Bool)
    YeohMaterial(props::MaterialProperties)

Yeoh hyperelasticity, `W = C1 (I₁ - 3) + C2 (I₁ - 3)² + C3 (I₁ - 3)³`. With `compressible = true`, `I₁` is replaced by
`Ĩ₁ = J^(-2/3) I₁` and the volumetric term `bulk/2 (ln J)²` is added; otherwise `bulk` is not used.

The `props` form uses `c1`, `c2`, `c3` and `bulk`; the key `compressible` enables the compressible form.
"""
YeohMaterial

@doc """
    MooneyMaterial(α₀, α₁, α₂, incompressible::Bool = true)
    MooneyMaterial(props::MaterialProperties)

Mooney-Rivlin hyperelasticity with the shear modulus `2α₁` and the bulk coefficient `α₀`. With `incompressible = true`
the stored energy is `W = α₁ (I₁ - 3) + α₂ (I₂ - 3)` and `α₀` is not used. Otherwise
`W = α₁ (Ĩ₁ - 3) + α₂ (Ĩ₂ - 3) + α₀/2 (J - 1)²`, with the invariants `Ĩ₁`, `Ĩ₂` of `J^(-2/3) C`.

The `props` form uses `alpha0`, `alpha1` and `alpha2`; the key `incompressible` enables the incompressible form.
"""
MooneyMaterial

@doc """
    ArrudaBoyceMaterial(C1, λm, bulk, compressible::Bool)
    ArrudaBoyceMaterial(props::MaterialProperties)

Arruda-Boyce eight-chain hyperelasticity.

The stored energy is a five-term series in the first invariant,
`W = C1 Σᵢ αᵢ βⁱ⁻¹ (I₁ⁱ - 3ⁱ)` for `i = 1…5`, with `β = λm⁻²` and
`α = (1/2, 1/20, 11/1050, 19/7000, 519/673750)`. `C1` is the initial shear stiffness and `λm` the locking stretch.
With `compressible = true`, `I₁` is replaced by `Ĩ₁ = J^(-2/3) I₁` and the term `bulk/2 ((J² - 1)/2 - ln J)` is
added; otherwise `bulk` is not used.

The `props` form uses `c1`, `lambdam` and `bulk`; the key `compressible` enables the compressible form.
"""
ArrudaBoyceMaterial

@doc """
    FplasticMaterial(E, ν, Hiso, Hkine, Y0, Yinf, Yexp, soft)
    FplasticMaterial(props::MaterialProperties)

Finite strain von Mises plasticity with logarithmic (Hencky) elasticity, solved by a radial return in the principal
Kirchhoff stresses.

The yield function is `‖dev(τ) - Q‖ - √(2/3) (Y0 - q)` with the Kirchhoff stress `τ`, the kinematic hardening stress
`Q` and the isotropic hardening stress `q = -K(α)`. `K(α) = Hiso α + (Yinf - Y0)(1 - exp(-Yexp α))` saturates at the
yield stress `Yinf`, and `Hkine` is the linear kinematic hardening modulus. `soft` is a thermal softening coefficient:
`Y0` and `Hiso` are scaled by `exp(-soft (θ - θref))` with the temperature `θ` of the material point.

The `props` form uses `young`, `poisson`, `isotropich`, `kinematich`, `yieldstress`, `yieldinf`, `hardexp` and
`softening`.
"""
FplasticMaterial

@doc """
    JohnsonCookMaterial(; E, nu, A, B, C, n, m, edot0, T, Tref, Tmelt, rho = 1.0)
    JohnsonCookMaterial(props::MaterialProperties)

Finite strain Johnson-Cook plasticity with hardening, rate dependence and thermal softening.

The yield stress is `√(2/3) (A + B εᵖⁿ) (1 + C ln(ε̇ᵖ/edot0)) (1 - ((T - Tref)/(Tmelt - Tref))ᵐ)`.

The temperature `T` belongs to the material: `setTemperature` on a point has no effect, so
build one material per temperature.

The `props` form uses the keys `young`, `poisson`, `density`, `a`, `b`, `c`, `n`, `m`, `edot0`,
`temp`, `modelreftemp` and `melttemp`. MUESLI's positional constructors are not exposed, as they
ignore the melting temperature and overwrite `edot0`.
"""
JohnsonCookMaterial

@doc """
    ZerilliArmstrongMaterial(; E, nu, C0, C1, C2, C3, C4, C5, n, T, a1 = 1.0, b1 = 1.0, rho = 1.0)
    ZerilliArmstrongMaterial(props::MaterialProperties)

Finite strain Zerilli-Armstrong plasticity, a rate and temperature dependent flow stress

    σ_y = √(2/3) [(C1 + C2 √εᵖ) exp(-C3 T + C4 T ln ε̇ᵖ) + C5 εᵖⁿ + C0]

`a1` and `b1` scale it with an ageing parameter; the defaults leave it unchanged. As for
[`JohnsonCookMaterial`](@ref), the temperature `T` belongs to the material.

The `props` form uses the keys `young`, `poisson`, `density`, `c0` to `c5`, `n`, `temp`, `a1`
and `b1`.
"""
ZerilliArmstrongMaterial

@doc """
    ViscoplasticNLMaterial(; E, nu, yield, Hiso, Hinf, H0, Hdelta, eta, exponent, alpha,
                           Hkine = 0.0, rho = 1.0, plasticity = "mises")
    ViscoplasticNLMaterial(props::MaterialProperties)

Von Mises viscoplasticity with saturating isotropic hardening. The yield function is `‖dev(σ) - Q‖ - √(2/3) (yield - q)`
with `q = -Hiso ξ - (Hinf - H0)(1 - exp(-Hdelta ξ))` and linear kinematic hardening `Hkine`. The plastic multiplier
follows the overstress law `Δγ = 2μ Δt/eta (f/(2μ))^exponent`. `alpha` is the thermal expansion coefficient.

The `props` form uses the keys `young`, `poisson`, `density`, `yieldstress`, `isotropich`, `isohardinf`,
`isohardzero`, `isoharddelta`, `kinematich`, `eta`, `viscousexponent` and `alpha`, plus the string `"plasticity"`
(see [`setString!`](@ref MuesliMaterials.setString!)).
"""
ViscoplasticNLMaterial

